      "text": "To fully simplify \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express the radicand in terms of powers of 2 and 3:**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Separate the expression inside the fourth root into parts that are powers of 2 and parts that are powers of 3:**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^7} \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{3^3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7}\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{2^7} = 2^{7/4} = 2^{1 + 3/4} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4}\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{2^7} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify \\(\\sqrt[4]{3^3}\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{3^3} = 3^{3/4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combine the simplified parts:**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Combine the exponents of the same bases inside the fourth root:**\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot 3^{3/4} \\cdot 2^{3/4} = 2^{1 + 3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2^{7/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Express the result in the form \\(a \\sqrt[4]{b}\\):**\n   \\[\n   2^{7/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2^{1 + 3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot (2 \\cdot 3)^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = 2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Identify \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):**\n   \\[\n   a = 2, \\quad b = 6^3 = 216\n   \\]\n\n9. **Calculate \\(a + b\\):**\n   \\[\n   a + b = 2 + 216 = 218\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{218}\n\\]",
