      "text": "We start by expressing the given expression $\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}$ in a form that allows us to simplify it easily. The expression can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite the expression using the property of exponents that states $\\sqrt[n]{a^m} = a^{m/n}$:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = (2^7 \\cdot 3^3)^{1/4}\n\\]\n\nNext, we apply the power of a product rule, which states $(xy)^n = x^n y^n$:\n\n\\[\n(2^7 \\cdot 3^3)^{1/4} = 2^{7/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite the exponents as mixed numbers:\n\n\\[\n2^{7/4} = 2^{1 + 3/4} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4}\n\\]\n\\[\n3^{3/4} = 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nCombining these, we get:\n\n\\[\n2^{7/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nWe can combine the terms that have the same base:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2^{1 + 3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2^{7/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2^{1 + 3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot (2 \\cdot 3)^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nSince $2^{3/4} \\cdot 6^{3/4} = (2 \\cdot 6)^{3/4} = 12^{3/4}$, we have:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite $2^{3/4} \\cdot 6^{3/4}$ as $(2 \\cdot 6)^{3/4} = 12^{3/4}$, so:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = 12^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the expression in the form $a \\sqrt[4]{b}$ is:\n\n\\[\na = 12, \\quad b = 1\n\\]\n\nSo, $a + b = 12 + 1 = 13$.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]",
