      "text": "We start by simplifying the expression \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\). To do this, we express the radicand in a form that allows us to separate the perfect fourth powers from the rest.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the expression inside the fourth root:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^6 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^3}\n\\]\nSince \\(2^6 = (2^2)^3 = 4^3\\), we can rewrite the expression as:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^6 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{4^3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^3}\n\\]\nNext, we need to separate the perfect fourth powers from the rest. We can write \\(4^3\\) as \\((2^2)^3 = 2^6\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^6 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^6 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^6 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{(2^2)^3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{(2^2 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3})^4} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^3}\n\\]\nSince \\(2^3 = 8\\) and \\(3^3 = 27\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^6 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 27} = \\sqrt[4]{4^3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 27} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{54}\n\\]\nThus, the simplified form of \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\) is \\(2 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{54}\\). Here, \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = 54\\). Therefore, the sum \\(a + b\\) is:\n\\[\na + b = 2 + 54 = 56\n\\]\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{56}\n\\]",
